{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MWeKX6H3KP9uZLuEjWu1qH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose tasks according to the constraints.\n\n## Step 1: Analyze Project Context\n\nThis is a specification-driven development project that re-implements Unix utilities in Go, verified against GNU reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n## Step 2: Summarize Project State\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge from reference behavior on edge cases. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations, and verifies via differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/` - Per-utility Go implementations\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Syscall abstractions (Darwin/Linux)\n\n\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specs, PRDs, use cases, test suites\n\n**Current state**: The testutils and two utilities (ts, cat) are fully implemented with tests, while the sys and format packages have specifications but no visible implementation code yet.\n\n**Release roadmap**: rel01.0 is in progress with the ts use case underway, and rel01.1 is pending with wc, sponge, and cat. Looking at what's actually been built, I see testutils, ts, and cat already have working code, which means the implementation is ahead of where the roadmap suggests.\n\nThe real gaps are in the packages and commands that only have specs: wc and sponge have no code yet, and neither do the sys and format packages. Since rel01.0 is the current focus and ts is already done, I should prioritize what's blocking that release from completion. in_progress). The code for ts and its tests exist. But the status is still \"in_progress\" meaning it's not yet complete/verified.\n\nHowever, looking at the existing source code, both ts and its tests are implemented. The testutils harness is implemented. So rel01.0 might be close to completion.\n\nNext would be rel01.1: wc, cat, sponge. Cat already has code. So remaining for rel01.1:\n- cmd/wc - needs implementation and tests\n- cmd/sponge - needs implementation and tests\n\nSince I can only propose 1 task (constraint: Do NOT exceed 1 tasks), I should focus on the most impactful next task.\n\nGiven that rel01.0 appears to have code and rel01.1 needs wc and sponge, I should tackle cmd/wc next. It has a complete PRD, doesn't depend on pkg/sys or pkg/format, and only needs pkg/testutils which already exists. Following the principle of splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks, I'll propose the implementation task first since that comes before tests. Looking at the wc requirements, I need to handle the default behavior with its four key items. I've got 22 requirement items across multiple categories, which is too many for a single code task. I should split this into separate implementation and test tasks per the guidelines. Looking at the existing codebase, I see that testutils, ts, and cat are already implemented, so for the wc command I need to figure out which requirements are most critical to tackle first given the one-task constraint. Now I'm mapping out the specific files I need to reference and the requirements breakdown for the wc implementation, starting with the main.go file and pulling patterns from the cat implementation for flag parsing and signal handling. I'm counting up the actual requirement items across all the R-groups—that's 22 total items, which is quite substantial. Looking at the cat implementation for reference, it handles all of prd006-cat in about 357 lines, so wc should be similarly scoped. Given the guideline that code tasks should target 5-8 requirements, I should organize this at the R-group level rather than breaking down individual sub-items. So I'm mapping this as a single task covering R1 through R6, with each R-group representing one major requirement area.\n\nNow I'm laying out the specific requirements: R1 handles the core counting behavior with proper output formatting, R2 manages all the flag combinations and their interactions, R3 deals with multi-file handling and alignment, R4 covers stdin and special input modes, R5 handles exit codes, and R6 adds the SIGPIPE handler for clean shutdown. That gives me six well-defined requirements that fit the target range.\n\nFor the design approach, I'm planning to follow the same patterns used in the cat implementation for flag parsing and signal handling, use manual parsing instead of the flag package to support both combined short flags and long options like --total and --files0-from, count actual newlines for the -l flag to match the standard wc behavior, handle character counting differently depending on locale settings, and compute column widths dynamically based on the largest count value across all input files. Now I'm examining the existing patterns in the cat implementation to inform my design approach. I'll follow the same SIGPIPE handling with a signal goroutine, use manual flag parsing to support combined short flags and GNU long options, count newlines as literal 0x0A bytes per the spec, and structure the code around a processFile function similar to what's already established.\n\nFor character counting, I need to handle UTF-8 properly by using RuneCount for valid sequences while treating invalid bytes individually. The output formatting requires dynamic column widths based on the maximum count across all files, with right-aligned columns and a total line for multi-file input.\n\nI've got eight acceptance criteria covering compilation, basic functionality with the example input producing the right counts, proper flag ordering, multi-file output with totals, the --total modes to suppress or show only totals, error handling for missing files, empty stdin behavior, and passing go vet checks.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the scope more carefully. Looking at the cat implementation which is around 357 lines for its requirements, wc should be comparable or slightly larger given the additional complexity around column alignment, total line modes, and character counting logic.\n\nI'm checking whether --files0-from would push this over the 350-line limit. Since the constraint is to stay within 250-350 lines and I can only propose one task, I need to decide whether to include it or scope it out. But the requirement boundary includes it as R4.4, so splitting feels artificial. I'll include the full scope and target around 350 lines, similar to what cat achieved.\n\nLet me verify there's no existing wc implementation in the codebase first.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":94811,"cache_read_input_tokens":18565,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":94811},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f452d70c-317b-4dbd-9b77-8eb44a5b6ace","uuid":"28ed5c6f-989f-4918-863c-a237460c5f8d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MWeKX6H3KP9uZLuEjWu1qH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is a specification-driven development experiment that re-implements classic Unix utilities in Go, verified against GNU reference binaries through differential testing. The goal is to stress-test the mage-claude-orchestrator code generation system.\n\n**Architecture**: Per-utility packages under `cmd/`, shared libraries under `pkg/` (testutils for differential testing, sys for syscall abstractions, format for output formatting), specs under `docs/`, and Mage build targets under `magefiles/`.\n\n**Current implementation state**:\n- `pkg/testutils` — implemented (difftest.go, normalize.go)\n- `cmd/ts` — implemented (main.go, ts_test.go)\n- `cmd/cat` — implemented (main.go, cat_test.go)\n- `cmd/wc` — PRD complete, use case and test suite written, no code\n- `cmd/sponge` — PRD complete, use case and test suite written, no code\n- `pkg/sys`, `pkg/format` — specs complete, no code\n\n**Current release**: rel01.0 (in_progress) has ts code complete. rel01.1 (pending) needs wc and sponge (cat is done).\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nThe earliest incomplete work is rel01.1, which needs cmd/wc and cmd/sponge implementations (cat is already done). cmd/wc depends only on pkg/testutils (already implemented) and has no dependency on pkg/sys or pkg/format per the dependency map. It is the next canonical task: implementation before tests, and wc sorts before sponge alphabetically.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement cmd/wc (prd005-wc R1-R6): count lines, words, and bytes\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd005-wc.yaml (full PRD for wc)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel01.1-uc001-wc.yaml (use case flow and success criteria)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel01.1.yaml (wc test cases for understanding expected behavior)\n      - cmd/cat/main.go (reference pattern for flag parsing, SIGPIPE handling, and file processing)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct -- understand the test interface the test file will use)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/wc/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"Full wc implementation: flag parsing, counting, multi-file output, exit codes\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement default counting behavior: when no flags are given, print line count, word count, and byte count for each input. Read from stdin when no file arguments are given. Print one line per input with counts followed by filename; omit filename for stdin. Print a 'total' line when more than one file is given. Per prd005-wc R1.1-R1.4.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement all counting flags: -l counts newline bytes (0x0A occurrences, not logical lines); -w counts words (maximal sequences of non-whitespace per unicode.IsSpace); -c counts bytes; -m counts characters (runes, with each invalid UTF-8 byte counting as one character) and takes precedence over -c in output; -L prints the length of the longest line measured in display columns with tab expansion to next multiple of 8. When flags are combined, print counts in fixed order: lines, words, chars-or-bytes, max-line-length, regardless of flag order. Per prd005-wc R2.1-R2.6.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement multi-file column alignment and total modes: right-align counts in columns wide enough for the largest count across all files. Support --total=auto (default: total when >1 file), --total=always (total even for one file), --total=only (only the total line, no per-file), --total=never (no total even for multiple files). The total line uses the label 'total'. Per prd005-wc R3.1-R3.3.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement stdin and special input handling: treat '-' as a filename argument meaning stdin. Handle binary input (arbitrary byte sequences) without corruption or panic. Handle empty input producing zero counts. Support --files0-from=FILE which reads NUL-delimited filenames from FILE; when FILE is '-', filenames are read from stdin. Per prd005-wc R4.1-R4.4.\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Implement exit codes: exit 0 when all inputs processed successfully; exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened or read (still process and print counts for successfully read files before exiting 1); exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout. Per prd005-wc R6.1-R6.3.\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"Install SIGPIPE handler so wc exits cleanly (exit 0) when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer (e.g., piping to head), matching the pattern used in cmd/cat/main.go.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow the same SIGPIPE handling pattern as cmd/cat/main.go: goroutine with signal.Notify on syscall.SIGPIPE calling os.Exit(0).\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use manual flag parsing (not Go's flag package) to support combined short flags (-lwc) and GNU-style long options (--total, --files0-from), matching the flag parsing pattern in cmd/cat/main.go.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"For -l, count occurrences of byte 0x0A, not logical lines. A file with no trailing newline has a newline count equal to the number of 0x0A bytes. This matches wc.c:862 behavior.\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"For -m character counting, use utf8.RuneCount for valid UTF-8. Each invalid byte counts as one character per prd005-wc R2.4. Under LC_ALL=C, -m and -c produce identical counts per prd005-wc R5.2.\"\n      - id: D5\n        text: \"Dynamic column width: compute from the maximum count value across all files and the total, right-aligning all count columns to that width. This matches GNU wc's dynamic column-width behavior per prd005-wc R3.1.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/wc/main.go compiles with 'go build ./cmd/wc/' with no errors.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"printf 'foo\\\\nbar baz\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/wc produces output matching GNU gwc format: counts '2 3 12' with appropriate spacing.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Combined flags (-w -l -c) produce counts in fixed order (lines, words, bytes) regardless of flag order on the command line.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"Two-file input produces per-file count lines and a 'total' line with right-aligned columns.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"--total=never with two files prints no total line; --total=only prints only the total line.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"Non-existent file argument prints error to stderr, exits 1, and still processes and prints counts for remaining valid files.\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"Empty stdin produces zero counts ('0 0 0' for default flags).\"\n      - id: AC8\n        text: \"'go vet ./cmd/wc/' passes with no diagnostics.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":94811,"cache_read_input_tokens":18565,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":94811},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f452d70c-317b-4dbd-9b77-8eb44a5b6ace","uuid":"17b2de9c-6677-43b7-b925-5c59ab9a5f30"}
